1
00:00:05,443 --> 00:00:06,343
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a simple question.

2
00:00:08,704 --> 00:00:10,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is pain a problem?

3
00:00:17,346 --> 00:00:18,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I don't want it.

4
00:00:21,427 --> 00:00:21,807
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want it.

5
00:00:21,827 --> 00:00:22,647
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which is first?

6
00:00:22,907 --> 00:00:30,889
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want it, thus it's a problem, or it's a problem, thus you don't want it?

7
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:31,450
[SPEAKER_00]: What is first?

8
00:00:31,590 --> 00:00:32,730
[SPEAKER_01]: My... Yeah.

9
00:00:33,801 --> 00:00:34,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's true.

10
00:00:34,702 --> 00:00:35,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is not wanted.

11
00:00:36,263 --> 00:00:37,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is a problem.

12
00:00:38,064 --> 00:00:39,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is the pain a problem that's not wanted?

13
00:00:43,368 --> 00:00:44,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I don't want it.

14
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:46,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

15
00:00:47,591 --> 00:00:50,913
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you make it a problem because you don't want it.

16
00:00:56,917 --> 00:00:57,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop

17
00:00:59,437 --> 00:01:06,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Resisting pain, as in stop trying to get rid of the pain, pain ceases to be a problem.

18
00:01:11,119 --> 00:01:13,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, it gets too much pain.

19
00:01:13,859 --> 00:01:14,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Too much.

20
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:16,200
[SPEAKER_00]: What is too much?

21
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:17,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you get too much pain?

22
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:20,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it depends what you mean by that.

23
00:01:20,361 --> 00:01:28,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you mean too much of painfulness of the pain, in which case, well, as long as you're still alive, it's not too much.

24
00:01:29,686 --> 00:01:32,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you mean, this is too much of a problem for me.

25
00:01:34,069 --> 00:01:34,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't want it.

26
00:01:35,830 --> 00:01:39,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't have a problem with pain.

27
00:01:39,873 --> 00:01:40,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

28
00:01:40,934 --> 00:01:42,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, there cannot be too much of a pain.

29
00:01:44,156 --> 00:01:48,379
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha gave that example of highway robbers sawing your limbs off one by one.

30
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:54,743
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the mind has ill will towards them, means it hasn't fulfilled the teaching.

31
00:01:57,521 --> 00:02:06,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by fulfilling the dhamma, it's inconceivable to have ill-will towards pain or those who cause it.

32
00:02:07,808 --> 00:02:08,969
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just inconceivable.

33
00:02:09,269 --> 00:02:14,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it was not conceivable to begin with, but it was your ignorance of it that was conceiving it.

34
00:02:17,795 --> 00:02:23,219
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, back to the same simple question, which is a reflection anybody can do.

35
00:02:24,909 --> 00:02:28,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is painful feeling a problem?

36
00:02:30,971 --> 00:02:33,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I would prefer pleasure.

37
00:02:34,033 --> 00:02:35,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so why would I prefer pleasure?

38
00:02:35,953 --> 00:02:36,493
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not pain.

39
00:02:37,814 --> 00:02:40,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why... why is it better then?

40
00:02:42,336 --> 00:02:46,858
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize you won't find an objective answer to it.

41
00:02:48,078 --> 00:02:52,720
[SPEAKER_01]: You have an attitude of not wanting pain and you have an attitude of wanting pleasure.

42
00:02:53,704 --> 00:02:55,345
[SPEAKER_01]: And your whole life is dictated by that.

43
00:02:56,286 --> 00:02:59,809
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at that attitude as to why is that attitude there, you won't find an answer.

44
00:03:00,849 --> 00:03:01,950
[SPEAKER_01]: It's Kṛṭṣṛṛa's attitude.

45
00:03:03,271 --> 00:03:12,858
[SPEAKER_01]: And that... that's the only thing, the only... one and only reason for human suffering of any kind.

46
00:03:14,579 --> 00:03:17,001
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant by craving means suffering.

47
00:03:17,582 --> 00:03:19,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of craving means absence of suffering.

48
00:03:20,724 --> 00:03:21,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is that attitude.

49
00:03:24,130 --> 00:03:25,331
[SPEAKER_01]: I want this, I don't want this.

50
00:03:26,771 --> 00:03:31,434
[SPEAKER_01]: But even if a person just starts reflecting, don't jump into answering right away.

51
00:03:31,514 --> 00:03:42,179
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just wise pain, any pain, physical, emotional, any pain, any disagreeable feeling that I'm experiencing, why is it not wanted by default?

52
00:03:42,679 --> 00:03:44,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that my default attitude?

53
00:03:53,469 --> 00:04:04,418
[SPEAKER_01]: And by putting yourself in such a question, without providing a rational answer, you're becoming aware of that problem, the problem of your attitude.

54
00:04:06,479 --> 00:04:12,704
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes, yeah, sometimes maybe you have to keep that question for days, as a way of contemplation.

55
00:04:12,724 --> 00:04:14,966
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to keep repeating it, just don't answer it.

56
00:04:15,166 --> 00:04:18,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep asking yourself, but look for an answer, but don't answer it.

57
00:04:19,743 --> 00:04:25,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're looking, but you're not allowing yourself to formulate it in that rational, psychologizing manner.

58
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:33,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you really want to become aware on that emotional level of your resistance to the unpleasant emotion.

59
00:04:34,419 --> 00:04:38,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can give this answer or that answer, but emotionally, you'll still be resisting it.

60
00:04:39,664 --> 00:04:42,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you would still want to prolong it, if it's pleasure.

61
00:04:45,843 --> 00:04:51,167
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is absolutely no justifiable reason for not wanting pain.

62
00:04:51,967 --> 00:05:00,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if your attitude towards your feelings matters, you would feel only what you want to feel.

63
00:05:01,794 --> 00:05:04,356
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, feelings would be obeying the attitude.

64
00:05:07,438 --> 00:05:14,944
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that you're experiencing pain, which is not wanted, means you have absolutely no say in the domain of feeling.

65
00:05:16,322 --> 00:05:22,087
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of feeling is unaware of your existence.

66
00:05:22,468 --> 00:05:23,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings feel.

67
00:05:24,329 --> 00:05:25,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasant feeling feels pleasure.

68
00:05:26,251 --> 00:05:28,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Unpleasant feeling feels displeasure.

69
00:05:29,074 --> 00:05:30,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral feeling feels neither.

70
00:05:30,895 --> 00:05:31,316
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

71
00:05:33,137 --> 00:05:33,998
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't exist for it.

72
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:41,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Your attitude is a self-assumed parasite upon it, because of which you suffer.

73
00:05:42,445 --> 00:05:47,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the pain, the experience of pain, does not experience the dukkha.

74
00:05:48,749 --> 00:05:52,611
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience of pain, you resisting it, you experience the dukkha.

75
00:05:52,791 --> 00:05:54,612
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you get to be free from suffering.

76
00:05:55,473 --> 00:05:58,195
[SPEAKER_01]: And your sense of self, and the gratuitous assumption, and everything else.

77
00:05:58,935 --> 00:06:01,757
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, pleasure, displeasure, and neutral remains.

78
00:06:03,078 --> 00:06:04,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they were never a problem.

79
00:06:07,140 --> 00:06:08,481
[SPEAKER_00]: The pain is inevitable.

80
00:06:09,485 --> 00:06:10,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

81
00:06:10,225 --> 00:06:12,906
[SPEAKER_01]: In as much as any feeling is inevitable for as long as you're alive.

82
00:06:13,526 --> 00:06:16,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Either pleasure, displeasure, or neither pleasure nor displeasure.

83
00:06:17,367 --> 00:06:20,127
[SPEAKER_00]: But the attitude is... Gratuitous.

84
00:06:21,227 --> 00:06:21,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

85
00:06:22,127 --> 00:06:22,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Optional.

86
00:06:23,548 --> 00:06:24,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not optional.

87
00:06:25,308 --> 00:06:28,229
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either you have attitude, or you have undone the attitude.

88
00:06:28,549 --> 00:06:29,169
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not optional.

89
00:06:30,289 --> 00:06:31,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe the type of attitude...

90
00:06:31,309 --> 00:06:31,969
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that... No.

91
00:06:32,109 --> 00:06:33,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the type of attitude is not optional.

92
00:06:34,830 --> 00:06:35,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Because...

93
00:06:35,928 --> 00:06:38,209
[SPEAKER_01]: In pleasure, you will always be wanting more pleasure.

94
00:06:38,329 --> 00:06:40,430
[SPEAKER_01]: In pain, you will always want to try and get rid of it.

95
00:06:41,050 --> 00:06:43,671
[SPEAKER_01]: In a neutral feeling, you're always going to distract yourself from it.

96
00:06:44,331 --> 00:06:47,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your attitudes are determined by the craving.

97
00:06:47,292 --> 00:06:49,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving has set outcomes.

98
00:06:54,318 --> 00:06:56,799
[SPEAKER_01]: What's optional is how you're going to go about it.

99
00:06:57,119 --> 00:06:58,800
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's already far down the line.

100
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:00,120
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not fundamental.

101
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:03,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamental is the attitude of wanting, not wanting.

102
00:07:03,281 --> 00:07:04,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamental is the attitude.

103
00:07:04,462 --> 00:07:05,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamental is craving.

104
00:07:06,062 --> 00:07:06,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving for what?

105
00:07:06,783 --> 00:07:08,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, craving for pleasure in more pleasure.

106
00:07:08,643 --> 00:07:09,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving for more pleasure in pleasure.

107
00:07:10,364 --> 00:07:12,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving for absence of pain in pain.

108
00:07:12,425 --> 00:07:15,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving for distraction, oblivion in either pleasant or unpleasant feeling.

109
00:07:19,155 --> 00:07:24,958
[SPEAKER_01]: But that attitude can become apparent if you start questioning yourself in regard to the presently enduring feeling.

110
00:07:24,998 --> 00:07:25,839
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I feeling now?

111
00:07:26,539 --> 00:07:29,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether something happened to you, somebody said something, you're upset.

112
00:07:30,001 --> 00:07:30,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain.

113
00:07:30,621 --> 00:07:40,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I need to... Well, first, you have to stop projecting the blame, so to speak, and responsibility onto the world for your pain.

114
00:07:42,733 --> 00:07:52,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Because as long as you still think that I'm suffering because such and such person said this and this, you have a lot of work to do, basically.

115
00:07:53,157 --> 00:07:54,617
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you have many views to undo.

116
00:07:56,418 --> 00:08:09,122
[SPEAKER_01]: When you take responsibility for how you're feeling and stop blaming the world, then you might be able to start discerning your attitude in regard to your own feeling as a primary and only, one and only source of your suffering.

117
00:08:10,607 --> 00:08:19,034
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, a person who is fully projected onto the world, being the reason for my pain, they can never see that, because they haven't taken responsibility for it.

118
00:08:25,139 --> 00:08:37,068
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say, you've taken responsibility for you being affected by your feelings, so you stop trying to, you know, talk things out with somebody who upsets you, nothing, like, you know,

119
00:08:40,266 --> 00:08:45,489
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's sort of done to, like, maintain social norms, that's fine, but you don't do it as a means of escape from your feeling.

120
00:08:46,749 --> 00:08:53,792
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize that responsibility for you being affected by what's being felt, is on you.

121
00:08:55,253 --> 00:09:03,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Not on the circumstances, not on the elements, not on other people, not of who said what, not of, oh, they didn't respect me enough, or they didn't do this, or they didn't do that.

122
00:09:03,657 --> 00:09:03,737
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

123
00:09:04,328 --> 00:09:14,916
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is you... you took for granted the attitude of trying to get rid of this unpleasure... unpleasurable feeling, and then, where you look, where would you go about it?

124
00:09:15,097 --> 00:09:15,597
[SPEAKER_01]: You... aha!

125
00:09:15,917 --> 00:09:16,538
[SPEAKER_01]: He said this.

126
00:09:16,778 --> 00:09:17,338
[SPEAKER_01]: It's his fault.

127
00:09:18,399 --> 00:09:18,940
[SPEAKER_01]: This happened.

128
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:19,860
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't have enough luck.

129
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:23,223
[SPEAKER_01]: It's everyone else's fault.

130
00:09:23,243 --> 00:09:28,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if people are genuinely rude and mean to you, if you suffer on account of it, that's on you.

131
00:09:30,460 --> 00:09:38,027
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said the wise monk, the one with the well-wisdom and right view, would endure harsh words, slander and all this.

132
00:09:38,507 --> 00:09:40,449
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you would endure elements.

133
00:09:41,009 --> 00:09:48,957
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you would endure heat, cold, hunger, insect bites, slanderous and abusive words.

134
00:09:49,137 --> 00:09:50,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's on the same level.

135
00:09:50,918 --> 00:09:51,639
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just elements.

136
00:09:53,300 --> 00:09:56,023
[SPEAKER_01]: But you being upset on account of it, that's a new point.

137
00:09:59,285 --> 00:10:01,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is pain a problem?

138
00:10:04,886 --> 00:10:21,889
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to reveal the attitude, your attitude, that makes it a problem, that feeds the attitude, that makes it a problem, that feeds the attitude, vicious circle, in order to reveal it, you have to stop blaming pain for a problem, for the problem.

139
00:10:23,449 --> 00:10:24,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is pain a problem?

140
00:10:25,410 --> 00:10:26,830
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, no,

141
00:10:27,753 --> 00:10:29,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I have a problem with pain?

142
00:10:31,695 --> 00:10:33,036
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a more accurate question.

143
00:10:34,136 --> 00:10:36,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Because pain in itself isn't the problem.

144
00:10:36,857 --> 00:10:38,678
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is a problem in regard to the pain.

145
00:10:39,799 --> 00:10:40,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

146
00:10:41,159 --> 00:10:42,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the pain is not wanted.

147
00:10:43,461 --> 00:10:43,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

148
00:10:44,781 --> 00:10:46,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is the pain not wanted?

149
00:10:46,502 --> 00:10:47,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I want it then.

150
00:10:47,382 --> 00:10:48,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, why would you want it then?

151
00:10:49,423 --> 00:10:52,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you have to either want or not want?

152
00:10:53,505 --> 00:10:56,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Why can you just be equanimous in regard to either?

153
00:11:00,885 --> 00:11:04,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I don't want pain, but I do not not want pain either.

154
00:11:09,769 --> 00:11:13,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Any pain, big or small, far or near, past, future, any.

155
00:11:15,391 --> 00:11:16,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude in regard to any pain.

156
00:11:19,413 --> 00:11:26,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop trying to get rid of it, then the attitude towards pleasure becomes also apparent.

157
00:11:27,616 --> 00:11:29,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop trying to entertain it.

158
00:11:29,961 --> 00:11:32,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop trying to make it stay longer.

159
00:11:32,182 --> 00:11:33,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop trying to get more of it.

160
00:11:35,224 --> 00:11:49,271
[SPEAKER_01]: So, automatically, by discerning the nature of your attitude towards pain, you also start discerning the nature of attitude towards pleasure, the gratuitous assumption of sensuality, sensual desire and pleasure you get from it.

161
00:11:50,232 --> 00:11:54,114
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean you stop perceiving agreeable and disagreeable things.

162
00:11:55,548 --> 00:11:56,328
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's one thing.

163
00:11:56,829 --> 00:12:01,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Another thing is craving for more of it, and craving for that absence, and craving for your distraction.

164
00:12:04,633 --> 00:12:12,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if a nature of pain ceases to be a problem for a person, they have nothing else to do.

165
00:12:14,199 --> 00:12:21,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it doesn't matter where it comes from, from what sense, from what thought, from what experience, from what circumstance.

166
00:12:22,503 --> 00:12:23,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is not resisted.

167
00:12:23,924 --> 00:12:25,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is not feared.

168
00:12:26,871 --> 00:12:28,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is not wanted either.

169
00:12:29,652 --> 00:12:35,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sometimes people would go the other way and overly indulge pain as a mean of showing that, you know... No.

170
00:12:36,254 --> 00:12:39,314
[SPEAKER_01]: If pain has arisen, it doesn't matter.

171
00:12:39,734 --> 00:12:40,615
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not a problem.

172
00:12:41,015 --> 00:12:41,975
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a secondary thing.

173
00:12:42,895 --> 00:12:49,037
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if pain arises on account of your restraint and determination and willpower, you have no problem with it.

174
00:12:53,038 --> 00:12:55,999
[SPEAKER_01]: If pain arises on account of somebody abusing you,

175
00:12:57,995 --> 00:12:59,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Offending you, whatever.

176
00:13:00,097 --> 00:13:01,178
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no problem with that either.

177
00:13:03,159 --> 00:13:05,882
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't allow yourself to be abused.

178
00:13:06,382 --> 00:13:10,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you allow yourself to be abused when you give in to the attitude towards pain.

179
00:13:10,766 --> 00:13:12,788
[SPEAKER_01]: You become abused.

180
00:13:12,828 --> 00:13:15,490
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

181
00:13:15,690 --> 00:13:24,498
[SPEAKER_01]: You, like, yeah, the circumstances are abusive, but you are not abused until you put the projection on the circumstances for that abuse.

182
00:13:26,648 --> 00:13:34,217
[SPEAKER_01]: If you realize that abuse... that abuse is founded upon your attitude towards the experienced feeling at the time.

183
00:13:35,158 --> 00:13:41,005
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if that attitude is attitude of, I don't want this, you made yourself affected by that abuse.

184
00:13:42,177 --> 00:13:48,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I mean, so sexual abuse and sexual... Well, how can you be sexually abused?

185
00:13:49,101 --> 00:13:51,822
[SPEAKER_01]: The body can be sexually abused, physically abused.

186
00:13:51,842 --> 00:13:55,304
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the kind of normal sexual relationship with people.

187
00:13:55,684 --> 00:13:56,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

188
00:13:56,244 --> 00:13:56,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

189
00:13:57,065 --> 00:14:00,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means that people gratuitously take the ownership of the body to be theirs.

190
00:14:01,787 --> 00:14:04,368
[SPEAKER_01]: But we all know how that ends.

191
00:14:04,388 --> 00:14:06,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Sickness, aging and death.

192
00:14:07,450 --> 00:14:09,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it was a mistake to take it as yours.

193
00:14:11,226 --> 00:14:13,467
[SPEAKER_01]: And you better undo that mistake while you're alive.

194
00:14:15,167 --> 00:14:15,787
[SPEAKER_01]: And you reflect.

195
00:14:16,107 --> 00:14:24,710
[SPEAKER_01]: If this body were truly mine, in the same sense that I said, if the feelings were truly yours, your attitude would be the first and your feelings would be determined by it.

196
00:14:25,970 --> 00:14:30,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Not your attitude would be determined by the corresponding feeling that has arisen.

197
00:14:30,952 --> 00:14:37,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, oh, so my attitude is secondary to this, yet it's acting like it's some sort of commander-in-chief.

198
00:14:40,743 --> 00:14:46,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So, me resisting pain, is my attitude towards the reason pain, which has a reason on its own.

199
00:14:46,924 --> 00:14:54,406
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I were to develop the attitude of, hold on, that's not a problem, and I stop gratuitously resisting it, you won't be able to suffer.

200
00:14:55,407 --> 00:14:59,548
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, the body gets, somebody attacks you, beats you up physically.

201
00:15:01,628 --> 00:15:02,428
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one thing.

202
00:15:03,628 --> 00:15:05,629
[SPEAKER_01]: But you, eh...

203
00:15:07,113 --> 00:15:12,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Through gratuitous assumption of the ownership of that body, that's how you become beaten up and abused.

204
00:15:16,217 --> 00:15:19,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter what type of assault and abuse it is.

205
00:15:19,878 --> 00:15:21,659
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, it can be done only to the body.

206
00:15:23,040 --> 00:15:28,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Only to the... literally to this bag of... bag of skin filled with lots of other organs and things.

207
00:15:32,344 --> 00:15:35,165
[SPEAKER_01]: And you thinking that you have a say,

208
00:15:37,408 --> 00:15:42,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Ultimate say, in what befalls that body, well, that was your ignorance from the start.

209
00:15:44,673 --> 00:15:53,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you never get physically abused, you will be abused, your body will be abused, by aging, sickness and death.

210
00:15:53,680 --> 00:15:54,821
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the ultimate abuse.

211
00:15:55,702 --> 00:16:01,386
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of physical abuse, if it doesn't kill you, can amount for the the abuse of death.

212
00:16:02,847 --> 00:16:04,268
[SPEAKER_01]: That is inevitable for everyone.

213
00:16:06,972 --> 00:16:21,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize that the attitude of abuse comes from you accepting the ownership of the body that has been abused, and you accept it through wanting pleasure through it, and you accept it through wanting to get rid of the pain through it.

214
00:16:24,716 --> 00:16:27,456
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it comes down again to the feelings that are present.

215
00:16:29,217 --> 00:16:30,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant feeling of abuse.

216
00:16:35,048 --> 00:16:40,169
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you don't even think twice before you start blaming, feeling what happened to the body and everything else.

217
00:16:44,070 --> 00:16:50,971
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you had enough mindfulness and self-reflection, you realize that, yeah, it's unfortunate, but that's the subject of elements.

218
00:16:52,771 --> 00:16:57,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Form is being deformed by elements, by other bodies, other things.

219
00:16:57,992 --> 00:16:59,012
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just nature of life.

220
00:16:59,112 --> 00:17:03,033
[SPEAKER_01]: By sickness, aging, death, whatever, accidents.

221
00:17:03,693 --> 00:17:04,673
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the nature of it.

222
00:17:07,235 --> 00:17:22,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was actually my mistake to assume that I had a say in that domain, to gratuitously entertain the attitude of saying no to this, or saying yes, I want more of this, whereby I have no say in arising of those things.

223
00:17:22,739 --> 00:17:26,520
[SPEAKER_01]: How can I have a say in prolonging it, or not arising in the first place?

224
00:17:30,701 --> 00:17:31,461
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you have a say

225
00:17:35,818 --> 00:17:49,209
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's unpleasant.

226
00:17:50,029 --> 00:17:50,990
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you suffer.

227
00:17:51,390 --> 00:17:53,312
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of the discrepancy, not because of the pain.

228
00:17:54,853 --> 00:17:59,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is the discrepancy that your attitude creates in regard to the present feeling.

229
00:18:00,077 --> 00:18:03,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the feeling is pleasurable, you suffer.

230
00:18:04,492 --> 00:18:05,413
[SPEAKER_01]: when you want more of it.

231
00:18:06,253 --> 00:18:11,576
[SPEAKER_01]: And the discrepancy there is pleasant feeling and wanting more of it, as in not enough of it.

232
00:18:13,197 --> 00:18:17,379
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to stop wanting more of it, then you're only going to have a pleasant feeling.

233
00:18:17,559 --> 00:18:19,200
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no discrepancy, no suffering.

234
00:18:20,961 --> 00:18:31,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Consequently, you have the unpleasant feeling present, pain is present, and you don't want that presence at all, which means you're overdoing it now.

235
00:18:31,867 --> 00:18:32,768
[SPEAKER_01]: There is some pain,

236
00:18:33,669 --> 00:18:36,090
[SPEAKER_01]: But your attitude does not want any of it.

237
00:18:36,651 --> 00:18:38,332
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another discrepancy you created it.

238
00:18:39,352 --> 00:18:40,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is pain.

239
00:18:40,193 --> 00:18:40,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is pain.

240
00:18:41,193 --> 00:18:41,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

241
00:18:42,954 --> 00:18:46,156
[SPEAKER_01]: If all you had is your attitude towards pain, you would be in control.

242
00:18:46,637 --> 00:18:48,198
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, there is the actual pain there.

243
00:18:48,858 --> 00:18:52,360
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's your attitude that contradicts it.

244
00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:55,142
[SPEAKER_01]: No, this pain must not be while the pain is.

245
00:18:57,243 --> 00:19:00,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this pleasure must be greater while it isn't.

246
00:19:02,758 --> 00:19:03,858
[SPEAKER_00]: This neutral feeling...

247
00:19:03,878 --> 00:19:06,779
[SPEAKER_01]: This neutral feeling... What neutral feeling?

248
00:19:07,579 --> 00:19:10,959
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is a neutral feeling, but you're not seeing any neutral feeling.

249
00:19:10,999 --> 00:19:12,000
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another discrepancy.

250
00:19:13,420 --> 00:19:16,760
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when you're bored, you feel unpleasant.

251
00:19:17,100 --> 00:19:17,480
[SPEAKER_01]: When there is...

252
00:19:17,881 --> 00:19:23,822
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're bored, basically, you feel neither pleasure nor unpleasure, but that in itself, it's unpleasant for you.

253
00:19:24,302 --> 00:19:30,683
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to feel something, because the threshold of your discernment of feeling is pain and pleasure.

254
00:19:30,763 --> 00:19:32,103
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even see the neutral one.

255
00:19:34,183 --> 00:19:35,144
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's unpleasant.

256
00:19:36,505 --> 00:19:39,446
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a slightly subtle discrepancy, but nevertheless, it's a discrepancy.

257
00:19:40,047 --> 00:19:42,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Probably, you know it's a presence.

258
00:19:42,448 --> 00:19:42,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

259
00:19:42,869 --> 00:19:44,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you stop contradicting it.

260
00:19:44,630 --> 00:19:47,392
[SPEAKER_01]: No contradiction, no discrepancy, no discrepancy, no suffering.

261
00:19:49,133 --> 00:19:54,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is a discrepancy created when your attitude clashes with the world, basically.

262
00:19:56,798 --> 00:19:59,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And world can present itself only in three forms.

263
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:03,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeable, disagreeable and neutral.

264
00:20:05,517 --> 00:20:09,600
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you deal on that level, you don't have to worry about anything else more particular than that.

265
00:20:16,204 --> 00:20:18,145
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how to deal with it on that level?

266
00:20:20,046 --> 00:20:23,148
[SPEAKER_01]: To take responsibility for whatever is being felt.

267
00:20:24,089 --> 00:20:31,553
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, this is felt by me because of my attitude towards it.

268
00:20:32,714 --> 00:20:34,235
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm making the feeling affect me.

269
00:20:34,677 --> 00:20:37,159
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling of pleasure, feeling of pain, neutral feeling.

270
00:20:38,280 --> 00:20:48,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody says something upsetting and the suffering, the pain arises, that's not... that pain is not in what's being said.

271
00:20:49,388 --> 00:20:50,529
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in that person.

272
00:20:50,849 --> 00:20:52,350
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in any of the circumstances.

273
00:20:52,971 --> 00:20:55,653
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in your attitude in regard to the circumstances.

274
00:20:59,115 --> 00:21:01,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to take responsibility for suffering, basically.

275
00:21:02,051 --> 00:21:09,897
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to take responsibility for agreeable and disagreeable, but you have to take responsibility for wanting agreeable and not wanting disagreeable.

276
00:21:10,498 --> 00:21:12,599
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean by taking responsibility?

277
00:21:13,060 --> 00:21:14,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Means seeing that it's on you.

278
00:21:14,761 --> 00:21:15,622
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no excuse.

279
00:21:15,762 --> 00:21:17,183
[SPEAKER_00]: It's my responsibility.

280
00:21:17,243 --> 00:21:17,943
[SPEAKER_01]: It's my doing.

281
00:21:18,644 --> 00:21:19,565
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm responsible for this.

282
00:21:19,945 --> 00:21:21,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when I suffer, I'm responsible for that.

283
00:21:22,487 --> 00:21:28,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Only through that attitude you can actually get a glimpse at least and maybe even, you know, go through with it.

284
00:21:29,152 --> 00:21:31,634
[SPEAKER_01]: You can figure out how to not suffer.

285
00:21:32,515 --> 00:21:46,287
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as your attitude doesn't take on suffering as your responsibility, and conflates suffering with disagreeable circumstances, with painful circumstances, you're not going to see the nature of suffering.

286
00:21:46,307 --> 00:21:47,488
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you free yourself from it?

287
00:21:49,289 --> 00:21:54,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to stop blaming painful circumstances for your suffering.

288
00:21:55,254 --> 00:21:56,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is not the pain.

289
00:21:59,288 --> 00:22:03,811
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is pain without suffering, but there cannot be suffering without pain.

290
00:22:07,314 --> 00:22:13,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you remove your attitude of denial and trying to get rid of the pain, you won't suffer.

291
00:22:14,940 --> 00:22:21,985
[SPEAKER_01]: If you remove your attitude and indulgent in regard to the pleasure, you won't suffer.

292
00:22:24,287 --> 00:22:28,490
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't suffer on account of either pleasure or pain or neutral feeling.

293
00:22:29,952 --> 00:22:41,800
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you do that... Well, the more you... like, this attitude is maintained through not knowing it, through gratuitously assuming it at face value and acting out of it.

294
00:22:41,820 --> 00:22:43,342
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to know that.

295
00:22:43,402 --> 00:22:43,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

296
00:22:44,382 --> 00:22:45,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's all you can do.

297
00:22:46,023 --> 00:22:48,005
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't decide to stop.

298
00:22:48,465 --> 00:22:52,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now I will stop trying to get rid of the feeling of unpleasure.

299
00:22:52,868 --> 00:22:53,609
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

300
00:22:54,925 --> 00:22:59,587
[SPEAKER_01]: But through understanding your attitude towards it, that attitude is being gradually cancelled out.

301
00:22:59,607 --> 00:23:00,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I feel pain?

302
00:23:01,807 --> 00:23:02,408
[SPEAKER_01]: I feel pain.

303
00:23:02,688 --> 00:23:04,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind jumps to blame the circumstances.

304
00:23:04,548 --> 00:23:05,329
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize that.

305
00:23:05,609 --> 00:23:06,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait a minute.

306
00:23:07,189 --> 00:23:08,470
[SPEAKER_01]: How many times has this happened?

307
00:23:08,570 --> 00:23:08,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Many times.

308
00:23:08,930 --> 00:23:09,830
[SPEAKER_01]: How many times will it happen?

309
00:23:10,110 --> 00:23:11,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have a say in circumstances?

310
00:23:12,711 --> 00:23:12,871
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

311
00:23:13,492 --> 00:23:21,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do I then keep sort of, like, inflaming myself with this attitude of, yeah, this or this or that person?

312
00:23:22,315 --> 00:23:22,655
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just...

313
00:23:23,312 --> 00:23:28,373
[SPEAKER_01]: It just feeds the fire further, while I kind of know already that that's not the problem.

314
00:23:29,034 --> 00:23:32,935
[SPEAKER_01]: That's really like indignation and anger and that needs constant maintenance.

315
00:23:33,735 --> 00:23:36,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Needs constant action to make it valid.

316
00:23:38,816 --> 00:23:39,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Constant blame.

317
00:23:41,037 --> 00:23:42,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Find a scapegoat for this.

318
00:23:42,877 --> 00:23:43,497
[SPEAKER_01]: It's his fault.

319
00:23:43,578 --> 00:23:44,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that false?

320
00:23:44,238 --> 00:23:45,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Who's gonna pay for this?

321
00:23:49,228 --> 00:23:55,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there are mistakes, and people do things that are unwise, and they're responsible for that.

322
00:23:55,650 --> 00:23:59,611
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you suffer on account of it, that responsibility is solely yours.

323
00:24:00,192 --> 00:24:02,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say the body is sick.

324
00:24:02,412 --> 00:24:02,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Sickness.

325
00:24:03,453 --> 00:24:04,153
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of pain.

326
00:24:05,493 --> 00:24:06,434
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a sickness, sure.

327
00:24:06,454 --> 00:24:09,815
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but you have to kind of, you know, sort yourself out, take some medicine.

328
00:24:10,375 --> 00:24:11,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's all fine.

329
00:24:11,695 --> 00:24:13,816
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be managing the circumstances.

330
00:24:14,608 --> 00:24:26,474
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't want to be doing it out of a... out of trying to get rid of the suffering, because that inquires you identifying suffering with disagreeable circumstances of an ailing body.

331
00:24:28,995 --> 00:24:31,396
[SPEAKER_01]: And how do you know whether you're acting out of it or not?

332
00:24:31,896 --> 00:24:32,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, ask yourself.

333
00:24:33,377 --> 00:24:35,358
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I don't take the medicine?

334
00:24:35,518 --> 00:24:37,639
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I don't go and see the doctor?

335
00:24:37,659 --> 00:24:41,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I going to get filled with anxiety and fear in regard to this?

336
00:24:41,781 --> 00:24:42,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I going to suffer?

337
00:24:43,606 --> 00:24:45,707
[SPEAKER_01]: And we're going to experience mental anguish, basically.

338
00:24:46,248 --> 00:24:52,132
[SPEAKER_01]: If the answer is yes, means you are still conflating suffering with circumstances.

339
00:24:52,912 --> 00:24:54,353
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just give up the medicines.

340
00:24:55,194 --> 00:24:55,734
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

341
00:24:56,014 --> 00:24:59,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, then you realize whether you give up the medicines or not, that's not the problem.

342
00:25:02,238 --> 00:25:09,363
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is you confusing your suffering with disagreeable feeling that has arisen on the level of an ailing body.

343
00:25:11,325 --> 00:25:17,331
[SPEAKER_01]: But you are making it into your mental language, because of the gratuitous attitude of trying to get rid of it.

344
00:25:18,872 --> 00:25:22,516
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you can take a step further and reflect, okay, so this is the work that needs to be done.

345
00:25:22,936 --> 00:25:25,999
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to do that work, I need to stay alive, so I'll take the medicine.

346
00:25:27,641 --> 00:25:31,064
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like, properly reflecting.

347
00:25:32,031 --> 00:25:36,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you take the medicine as like a borrowing the time, so that you can uproot the problem.

348
00:25:36,412 --> 00:25:40,913
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't take the medicine out of that blind... blind sort of just jump into it.

349
00:25:41,793 --> 00:25:42,233
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sick.

350
00:25:42,913 --> 00:25:43,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Fear.

351
00:25:43,733 --> 00:25:44,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Deal with it.

352
00:25:44,754 --> 00:25:45,954
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, and then I'll practice.

353
00:25:46,434 --> 00:25:48,715
[SPEAKER_00]: You got to look after your tools, kind of thing.

354
00:25:48,755 --> 00:25:50,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course, of course, exactly.

355
00:25:51,555 --> 00:25:58,677
[SPEAKER_01]: The body is a tool, on account of which you can understand the nature of the body, the nature of non-ownership, and so on.

356
00:25:59,157 --> 00:26:00,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, you need it.

357
00:26:02,065 --> 00:26:06,867
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why we say he wisely avoids a wild elephant, the one who practices dhamma.

358
00:26:06,887 --> 00:26:16,971
[SPEAKER_01]: He wisely avoids a snake, a charcoal pit, a pack of dogs, because he would be foolish, before he has completed the work, to ignore the fact that this might kill him.

359
00:26:20,373 --> 00:26:27,356
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't, you know, you don't go around with an attitude of health is good in itself, as in the health is the greatest value.

360
00:26:28,276 --> 00:26:29,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're going to be sorely disappointed.

361
00:26:32,767 --> 00:26:34,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So, health is basically a tool.

362
00:26:36,309 --> 00:26:43,794
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, okay, my health is not perfect, but the tool is still good enough.

363
00:26:43,874 --> 00:26:50,278
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can also then realize that you're able to go quite far with the attitude that it's good enough.

364
00:26:51,359 --> 00:26:52,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is good enough.

365
00:26:53,320 --> 00:26:58,863
[SPEAKER_01]: And it comes down to, well, as long as I'm still alive, and, you know, my senses are still there, it's still good enough.

366
00:27:00,044 --> 00:27:01,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're in pain and discomfort.

367
00:27:07,040 --> 00:27:10,423
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to go and seek out intense feelings.

368
00:27:11,184 --> 00:27:12,645
[SPEAKER_01]: No, absolutely not.

369
00:27:12,805 --> 00:27:13,626
[SPEAKER_01]: No, of course not.

370
00:27:13,846 --> 00:27:15,027
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only changing my attitude.

371
00:27:17,009 --> 00:27:17,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you do that?

372
00:27:19,111 --> 00:27:24,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if somebody has that thought, where would that action intention be rooted?

373
00:27:25,057 --> 00:27:25,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Fear of suffering.

374
00:27:26,478 --> 00:27:27,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Right there and then.

375
00:27:28,745 --> 00:27:36,707
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, I don't need to go out, because I just now revealed to myself that I'm trying to get rid of the suffering by thinking I'll go out and do this and this.

376
00:27:38,247 --> 00:27:42,569
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where you get rid of the suffering, where you recognize your attitude in regard to the disagreeable feeling.

377
00:27:42,949 --> 00:27:45,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is always... well, there's a feeling always present.

378
00:27:47,330 --> 00:27:47,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

379
00:27:47,590 --> 00:27:49,250
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the work is always present.

380
00:27:49,270 --> 00:27:55,992
[SPEAKER_01]: The work is always present in the sense of being mindful of the attitude towards the present feeling.

381
00:27:57,277 --> 00:28:02,000
[SPEAKER_01]: And in order to be mindful of the present feeling, you have to stop blaming the circumstances for the arising of that feeling.

382
00:28:03,020 --> 00:28:04,621
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to take responsibility for it.

383
00:28:05,041 --> 00:28:06,342
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to feel it, basically.

384
00:28:07,183 --> 00:28:09,044
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to allow it to be felt.

385
00:28:09,104 --> 00:28:10,044
[SPEAKER_00]: Not trying to get rid of it.

386
00:28:10,244 --> 00:28:13,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindful of the craving, the attitude.

387
00:28:13,867 --> 00:28:16,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can't be mindful of the craving as I'm craving and craving and craving.

388
00:28:16,788 --> 00:28:20,430
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be mindful of the feeling and then discern the nature of it.

389
00:28:20,791 --> 00:28:22,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, well, can I?

390
00:28:22,572 --> 00:28:23,032
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said,

391
00:28:23,690 --> 00:28:25,191
[SPEAKER_01]: My attitude is always second to this.

392
00:28:25,531 --> 00:28:28,073
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no... it's inconceivable to be different.

393
00:28:30,835 --> 00:28:39,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Once that which you have been prioritized, which you have been prioritizing, is seen as second, it's not a priority anymore.

394
00:28:41,362 --> 00:28:44,624
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that attitude is not a priority, that's how the attitude starts to fade.

395
00:28:46,726 --> 00:28:50,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through being mindful of the enduring feeling, you get to discern the nature of the feeling.

396
00:28:50,929 --> 00:28:52,870
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why mindfulness is the basis of knowledge.

397
00:28:54,321 --> 00:29:03,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like pain of restraint, saying no to yourself, saying no to sensual indulgence, saying no to laziness, saying no to ill-will, acting out of ill-will.

398
00:29:05,050 --> 00:29:06,171
[SPEAKER_01]: It produces pain.

399
00:29:07,992 --> 00:29:11,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is that discrepancy of I want to do this, but you say no.

400
00:29:12,536 --> 00:29:15,859
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, where is that discrepancy really rooted?

401
