[SPEAKER_01]: Negative attitude.

455
00:31:30,588 --> 00:31:32,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Negative attitude means sensuality has arisen.

456
00:31:32,749 --> 00:31:36,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, go and eat all these cakes that you want now.

457
00:31:36,271 --> 00:31:36,871
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a thought.

458
00:31:36,891 --> 00:31:38,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not actually eating the cakes.

459
00:31:40,196 --> 00:31:41,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're still not doing anything.

460
00:31:42,177 --> 00:31:44,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your response is a negative response.

461
00:31:44,479 --> 00:31:45,939
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, not a bad response.

462
00:31:45,979 --> 00:31:48,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Not a bad response in a negative, like, oh, it's bad.

463
00:31:48,461 --> 00:31:48,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

464
00:31:48,841 --> 00:31:51,103
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it means it's basically not a positive action.

465
00:31:51,443 --> 00:31:51,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

466
00:31:52,003 --> 00:31:54,384
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a... it's a call to a positive action.

467
00:31:55,165 --> 00:32:00,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Go and... go and get these cakes and eat them and enjoy the sense of taste you get from it.

468
00:32:01,148 --> 00:32:07,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, all of that is still your thought and you are not eating it, you're not making any effort.

469
00:32:08,580 --> 00:32:09,981
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a negative experience.

470
00:32:10,081 --> 00:32:14,783
[SPEAKER_01]: It's absence of these cakes that will provide me with these great pleasures.

471
00:32:16,503 --> 00:32:17,744
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the pain of it.

472
00:32:20,965 --> 00:32:27,768
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because of your attitude that always welcomes greater pleasure from the currently experienced feeling.

473
00:32:29,209 --> 00:32:34,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the prospect of greater pleasure immediately is experienced as that great discrepancy.

474
00:32:37,490 --> 00:32:46,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And then people would give in, go and get all these cakes and overeat and whatnot, because they wanted to avoid the pain in the first place.

475
00:32:50,213 --> 00:32:56,134
[SPEAKER_01]: But can you really avoid, as in, can you really prevent the pain in the first place by doing that?

476
00:32:56,654 --> 00:32:59,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Or can you just covering it up and distract yourself from it?

477
00:33:02,176 --> 00:33:03,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize you can't avoid it.

478
00:33:04,503 --> 00:33:21,488
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that by giving in value to the joy that comes out of taste, through eating all these cakes in this example, you gave in the value, you confirmed that you were affected by it in the first place, by that pain of not having that sensuality.

479
00:33:25,550 --> 00:33:29,871
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as we said before, people think sensuality is unpleasant because they say no to it.

480
00:33:38,048 --> 00:33:38,988
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's other way around.

481
00:33:40,229 --> 00:33:53,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Through saying no to sensuality, you realize that the pain that you've been attributing to you saying no, as in not giving in to the positive action, the pain of it is basically in that positive action.

482
00:33:54,413 --> 00:34:03,216
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to get rid of sensuality and pain of sensuality and dependence and pressure and burden and all of it, you just need to stick to saying no to it.

483
00:34:04,316 --> 00:34:05,997
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the nature of it will become apparent.

484
00:34:08,863 --> 00:34:10,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not doing anything now.

485
00:34:11,626 --> 00:34:14,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And the thought of sensuality comes.

486
00:34:14,369 --> 00:34:18,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Restraint is closer to the natural state you were before.

487
00:34:18,713 --> 00:34:19,774
[SPEAKER_01]: You were not doing anything.

488
00:34:21,215 --> 00:34:24,979
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the thought of sensuality that pressures you to go and do it.

489
00:34:27,341 --> 00:34:29,343
[SPEAKER_01]: And the pain arises on account of that.

490
00:34:30,124 --> 00:34:32,026
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the discrepancy that's being created.

491
00:34:34,832 --> 00:34:39,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you were not doing anything before sensuality and you're not doing anything when you're restraining sensuality.

492
00:34:41,517 --> 00:34:42,917
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the constant there.

493
00:34:43,378 --> 00:34:51,443
[SPEAKER_01]: What has changed is absence of sensuality before the pain and then presence of sensuality when the pain is there.

494
00:34:52,424 --> 00:34:58,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, wait a minute, it's the presence or absence of sensuality craving for that pleasure

495
00:35:00,651 --> 00:35:06,014
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what creates the discrepancy of pain, of suffering, on account of sensuality, not my restraint.

496
00:35:06,415 --> 00:35:19,743
[SPEAKER_01]: My restraint is more like emulation of the proper behavior, of the proper action, of the action that doesn't give in to the pressure of sensuality or your will and so on.

497
00:35:20,123 --> 00:35:26,287
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the restraint, self-composure, virtue has to come first, because it starts emulating things rightly.

498
00:35:26,907 --> 00:35:27,768
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know it yet,

499
00:35:28,363 --> 00:35:30,845
[SPEAKER_01]: But on that ground, you can get to know it.

500
00:35:38,930 --> 00:35:43,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So, inevitably, pain is going to be felt when you say no to sensuality.

501
00:35:44,013 --> 00:35:44,953
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to accept it.

502
00:35:46,554 --> 00:35:55,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you accept it, then you can say, basically, to the pain and to yourself, that that pain belongs to this sensuality, not to the restraint of it.

503
00:35:57,162 --> 00:35:59,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I said restraint is that negative.

504
00:35:59,383 --> 00:36:00,264
[SPEAKER_01]: It's no action there.

505
00:36:01,365 --> 00:36:02,605
[SPEAKER_01]: It's abstaining from action.

506
00:36:04,287 --> 00:36:08,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you've been abstaining from sensual action when there was no sensuality pressuring you.

507
00:36:08,509 --> 00:36:09,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, sensuality is pressuring you.

508
00:36:09,890 --> 00:36:10,971
[SPEAKER_01]: You're practicing restraint.

509
00:36:11,691 --> 00:36:14,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you're on the same level of abstaining from that action.

510
00:36:15,554 --> 00:36:16,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, there is pain.

511
00:36:17,395 --> 00:36:18,035
[SPEAKER_01]: What's different?

512
00:36:18,216 --> 00:36:20,237
[SPEAKER_01]: It's absence or presence of sensuality.

513
00:36:21,758 --> 00:36:23,319
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it becomes even more apparent.

514
00:36:23,339 --> 00:36:26,261
[SPEAKER_01]: The one thing for sure, you'll never find peace

515
00:36:27,175 --> 00:36:45,741
[SPEAKER_01]: And, like, ultimate freedom, in the sense of not being pressured by circumstances, if you keep engaging with sensuality, with gratuitous cravings of pleasure through your senses, with gratuitous expressions of your ill-will through your senses.

516
00:36:49,942 --> 00:36:56,824
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether sensuality touches you, whether ill-will touches you, whether boredom touches you, what do they all have in common?

517
00:36:57,780 --> 00:36:58,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain.

518
00:36:59,521 --> 00:37:01,302
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a disagreeable experience.

519
00:37:04,183 --> 00:37:09,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Having an attitude of wanting to get rid of disagreeable means what?

520
00:37:10,967 --> 00:37:13,468
[SPEAKER_01]: In sensuality, how do you get rid of disagreeable?

521
00:37:13,728 --> 00:37:14,769
[SPEAKER_01]: By saying yes to it.

522
00:37:17,571 --> 00:37:19,892
[SPEAKER_01]: In ill-will, how do you get rid of the disagreeable?

523
00:37:21,533 --> 00:37:24,034
[SPEAKER_01]: By affecting the person or whatever circumstances.

524
00:37:24,194 --> 00:37:25,695
[SPEAKER_01]: By saying yes to it, exactly.

525
00:37:26,514 --> 00:37:28,816
[SPEAKER_01]: In boredom, how do you get rid of disagreeable?

526
00:37:29,136 --> 00:37:32,059
[SPEAKER_01]: By saying yes to it and finding things to distract yourself from it.

527
00:37:32,880 --> 00:37:41,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you have a positive attitude that result in positive actions of indulgence, ill-will, distraction, greed, aversion, delusion.

528
00:37:43,109 --> 00:37:51,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Not metaphysical things, the things that your attitudes are based on, your actions are based on, because of your attitude in regard to how you're feeling.

529
00:37:55,648 --> 00:37:57,229
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, that's what the Buddha meant.

530
00:37:57,569 --> 00:38:00,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever it's felt, counts as suffering.

531
00:38:02,271 --> 00:38:05,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, feeling of agreeable, pressures you.

532
00:38:06,754 --> 00:38:08,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling of disagreeable, pressures you.

533
00:38:09,035 --> 00:38:10,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling of neutral, pressures you.

534
00:38:12,677 --> 00:38:16,458
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that pressure is not in the feeling or on the feeling.

535
00:38:16,579 --> 00:38:18,379
[SPEAKER_01]: That pressure is in your ignorance of it.

536
00:38:20,080 --> 00:38:23,282
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are pressured in regard to pleasure, because you haven't understood it.

537
00:38:24,088 --> 00:38:27,430
[SPEAKER_01]: You are pressured in regard to this pleasure, because you haven't understood it.

538
00:38:29,350 --> 00:38:30,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes for the neutral feeling.

539
00:38:31,171 --> 00:38:32,172
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what Guruji said.

540
00:38:32,592 --> 00:38:35,913
[SPEAKER_01]: If the neutral feeling is not understood, it's felt unpleasantly.

541
00:38:36,113 --> 00:38:36,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is there.

542
00:38:37,374 --> 00:38:40,015
[SPEAKER_01]: If the neutral feeling is understood, there is no pressure.

543
00:38:40,275 --> 00:38:41,436
[SPEAKER_01]: It's felt pleasantly.

544
00:38:47,058 --> 00:38:50,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want sensuality in order to be free from pain.

545
00:38:51,535 --> 00:38:54,577
[SPEAKER_01]: You have ill will and anger in order to be free from pain.

546
00:38:56,439 --> 00:39:00,902
[SPEAKER_01]: You have distraction, boredom, indolence, laziness in order to be free from pain.

547
00:39:03,924 --> 00:39:05,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting to be free from pain?

548
00:39:09,528 --> 00:39:19,155
[SPEAKER_01]: If you stop equating that with trying to get rid of this pain, then you can actually learn how to free yourself from pain, from being affected by the pain.

549
00:39:22,703 --> 00:39:31,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will truly be free from pain, not when pain stops arising, but when your attitude towards pain changes.

550
00:39:35,452 --> 00:39:38,354
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's again just to also add to what you said.

551
00:39:38,374 --> 00:39:40,375
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to go out seeking out suffering and pain.

552
00:39:40,896 --> 00:39:44,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Just say no to your desires, and the pain is there.

553
00:39:45,386 --> 00:39:49,449
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to go and risk your life and feel threatened and get, you know, that existential dread going.

554
00:39:49,849 --> 00:39:58,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Just start saying no to your daily sense desires, and you'll see how quickly the pain will come knocking on your door.

555
00:40:01,958 --> 00:40:02,418
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

556
00:40:02,478 --> 00:40:12,306
[SPEAKER_01]: If the attitude is developed towards pain is not the problem, it's my attitude towards pain that's problem, then no amount of pain can prevail over the mind.

557
00:40:13,128 --> 00:40:15,369
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it would have meant for an arahant.

558
00:40:15,869 --> 00:40:18,650
[SPEAKER_01]: The disagreeable feelings cannot overwhelm his mind.

559
00:40:18,890 --> 00:40:20,551
[SPEAKER_01]: He is the one who overwhelms.

560
00:40:21,431 --> 00:40:23,472
[SPEAKER_01]: His mind overwhelms the disagreeable feelings.

561
00:40:24,852 --> 00:40:28,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Or he is the one who overwhelms his mind in regard to disagreeable feelings.

562
00:40:28,213 --> 00:40:35,195
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not just that he needs to do that as like some sort of, you know, a determination, sort of willpower practice.

563
00:40:35,496 --> 00:40:35,656
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

564
00:40:35,756 --> 00:40:39,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Through understanding, that's just a natural state of affairs.

565
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:46,061
[SPEAKER_01]: When disagreeable arises, it has arisen overwhelmed by the mind that has understood it.

566
00:40:46,101 --> 00:40:50,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, understanding overwhelms the disagreeable.

567
00:40:50,962 --> 00:40:56,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of understanding automatically is higher than disagreeable, or agreeable, or neutral.

568
00:40:56,743 --> 00:40:59,383
[SPEAKER_01]: So, none of those feelings can overwhelm it anymore.

569
00:40:59,843 --> 00:41:09,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not on the level of just I'm doing this, it's on the level of this, this thing, this pain that's there, there is the understanding of that pain that's there, simultaneously with that pain.

570
00:41:15,939 --> 00:41:16,919
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand what's there.

571
00:41:17,660 --> 00:41:19,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that understanding depends on what's there.

572
00:41:19,561 --> 00:41:22,023
[SPEAKER_01]: But whenever that thing is there, it has been understood.

573
00:41:23,023 --> 00:41:23,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Right there and then.

574
00:41:23,924 --> 00:41:24,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's there.

575
00:41:29,007 --> 00:41:31,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you theorized about it and rationalized about it.

576
00:41:31,689 --> 00:41:31,769
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

577
00:41:31,789 --> 00:41:32,569
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because it's there.

578
00:41:32,990 --> 00:41:33,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what's been understood.

579
00:41:34,090 --> 00:41:35,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of that thing.

580
00:41:35,852 --> 00:41:37,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of agreeable feeling.

581
00:41:37,733 --> 00:41:39,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of disagreeable feeling.

582
00:41:40,755 --> 00:41:44,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's just another way of saying there is no more attitude.

583
00:41:45,600 --> 00:41:53,107
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no more assumed, almost entitled attitude in regard to pleasure, pain, or neutral.

584
00:41:55,168 --> 00:42:02,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, pleasure feels pleasantly, displeasure feels unpleasantly, and neutral feelings feel neutrally.

585
00:42:03,455 --> 00:42:05,077
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no suffering in regard to any.

586
00:42:29,883 --> 00:42:32,085
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we started by saying, why is pain a problem?

587
00:42:32,906 --> 00:42:34,587
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I making a problem out of pain?

588
00:42:34,887 --> 00:42:35,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

589
00:42:35,988 --> 00:42:39,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I making a problem out of pleasure?

590
00:42:41,653 --> 00:42:42,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, well, I'm not making a problem.

591
00:42:42,854 --> 00:42:43,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Where you are?

592
00:42:44,316 --> 00:42:45,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are pressured by it.

593
00:42:47,398 --> 00:42:53,204
[SPEAKER_01]: If pleasure is truly not a problem, you wouldn't suffer on account of not getting enough of it.

594
00:42:54,585 --> 00:42:56,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Whichever shape or form pleasure would arise,

595
00:42:58,810 --> 00:43:00,551
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be self-sufficient to that extent.

596
00:43:02,853 --> 00:43:05,514
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, there would be no craving for more sensual pleasure.

597
00:43:08,196 --> 00:43:14,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have a thought of, as we said, of going and eating up all the nice sweet cakes.

598
00:43:17,221 --> 00:43:18,762
[SPEAKER_01]: That thought is agreeable.

599
00:43:19,443 --> 00:43:22,284
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nice, nice cakes, nice, nice taste.

600
00:43:25,686 --> 00:43:26,167
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's it.

601
00:43:26,767 --> 00:43:28,128
[SPEAKER_01]: In itself, that's sufficient.

602
00:43:29,483 --> 00:43:33,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would now that thought pressure you to go and make all these actions?

603
00:43:34,764 --> 00:43:37,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Finding cake, getting cake, eating cake, consuming cake, all of that.

604
00:43:40,285 --> 00:43:44,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you were affected by that pressure on the level of your thought.

605
00:43:47,107 --> 00:43:57,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you were affected, the only means of you dealing with being pressured and experiencing disagreeable, is seeking out what it sort of points at.

606
00:43:58,293 --> 00:44:01,195
[SPEAKER_01]: The cake at the mouth and eating, shoving cake in your mouth, all that.

607
00:44:03,717 --> 00:44:08,221
[SPEAKER_01]: But all that's secondary to the thought of that pleasure of the taste.

608
00:44:09,942 --> 00:44:16,807
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were indifferent to the pleasure of the taste on the level of the thought, committing actions on account of it becomes inconceivable.

609
00:44:17,587 --> 00:44:23,112
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean that you don't recognize this is a tasty cake and this is not a tasty food.

610
00:44:24,733 --> 00:44:27,175
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that experience can then

611
00:44:28,167 --> 00:44:35,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only reason you'll be going there and doing this and eating, because the context of

612
00:44:50,280 --> 00:44:57,264
[SPEAKER_01]: The thought of consuming a tasty food is still there, giving the meaning to all of your individual actions of getting there.

613
00:44:57,844 --> 00:45:02,347
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, once you go and eat the cake, you never can fulfill that thought.

614
00:45:03,087 --> 00:45:05,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Because thought is a thought, and taste is a taste.

615
00:45:05,969 --> 00:45:07,029
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a completely different sense.

616
00:45:07,990 --> 00:45:11,812
[SPEAKER_01]: But you were assuming this identity of the whole projection, the whole action.

617
00:45:13,353 --> 00:45:17,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Thought of a pleasant taste, consumption of the pleasant taste, it's identified.

618
00:45:18,212 --> 00:45:19,352
[SPEAKER_01]: But deep down, it isn't.

619
00:45:19,493 --> 00:45:20,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that.

620
00:45:21,293 --> 00:45:22,534
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's obvious to everyone.

621
00:45:22,634 --> 00:45:28,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody will say, people who never read Buddhism practically, yeah, you know, like, you can't really satisfy your desires, can't you?

622
00:45:29,316 --> 00:45:30,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's nothing to satisfy.

623
00:45:33,578 --> 00:45:36,659
[SPEAKER_01]: There was a pleasure, initial pleasure on the level of the thought.

624
00:45:36,839 --> 00:45:37,239
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

625
00:45:38,340 --> 00:45:42,401
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's your attitude that automatically wants more pleasure.

626
00:45:42,861 --> 00:45:44,182
[SPEAKER_01]: So you think more about it.

627
00:45:47,166 --> 00:45:50,189
[SPEAKER_01]: That wants more pleasure, so you go and do something about it.

628
00:45:53,011 --> 00:46:02,320
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, it's attitude of wanting more pleasure in regard to the thought of pleasure, that's the discrepancy that's being created there.

629
00:46:03,140 --> 00:46:04,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that attitude is why you suffer.

630
00:46:05,262 --> 00:46:08,065
[SPEAKER_01]: That attitude is why you assume the whole world of sensuality.

631
00:46:19,482 --> 00:46:40,112
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you undo the attitude of automatically wanting pleasure and automatically not wanting pain, then you have an agreeable thought of some nice, very, like, tasty food, or agreeable thought of some beautiful music, or agreeable thought of beautiful sights.

632
00:46:40,892 --> 00:46:41,232
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

633
00:46:41,952 --> 00:46:45,034
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just beautiful things that you thought about.

634
00:46:47,618 --> 00:46:50,139
[SPEAKER_01]: And that agreeability of them, stays with them.

635
00:46:51,359 --> 00:46:53,379
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no attitude of you, I want more of this.

636
00:46:54,880 --> 00:46:59,321
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, in the thought, there will be only the thought.

637
00:47:00,141 --> 00:47:02,261
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sight, there will be only the sight.

638
00:47:03,162 --> 00:47:08,383
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sound, you hear something pleasant, that pleasure is confined to that pleasure.

639
00:47:10,183 --> 00:47:14,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't interfere with it, through your attitude of wanting more of it.

640
00:47:15,608 --> 00:47:18,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't interfere with it through an attitude of trying to get rid of it.

641
00:47:20,832 --> 00:47:25,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the sin, there'll just be the sin, in the hurt, there'll just be the hurt, the bahir-sutta, the famous bahir-sutta.

642
00:47:27,156 --> 00:47:29,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you got so absorbed in the sin that there's only the sin.

643
00:47:30,999 --> 00:47:33,461
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's because in the sin, there can only be the sin.

644
00:47:34,302 --> 00:47:36,743
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have the something else in the sin.

645
00:47:37,664 --> 00:47:37,804
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

646
00:47:38,325 --> 00:47:40,866
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's there, it's sin.

647
00:47:40,886 --> 00:47:43,028
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have the hurt in the sin, or the thought in the sin.

648
00:47:45,756 --> 00:47:47,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Inasmuch you can't have the scene in the thought.

649
00:47:49,199 --> 00:47:50,020
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I'm saying here.

650
00:47:50,340 --> 00:47:52,282
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sensuality is on the level of the mind.

651
00:47:54,384 --> 00:47:55,445
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I said.

652
00:47:55,505 --> 00:48:04,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Even for the Tāgata, the beautiful things remain in the world, but it's the desire and lust, his thought, and desire and lust that's been perpetuated through it.

653
00:48:05,495 --> 00:48:06,596
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what he has uprooted.

654
00:48:16,665 --> 00:48:22,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a thought of sensual pleasure is agreeable on the level of the thought.

655
00:48:25,911 --> 00:48:30,554
[SPEAKER_01]: And you recognize that is... that is the extent of its gratification.

656
00:48:31,634 --> 00:48:33,876
[SPEAKER_00]: That... that initial agreeability.

657
00:48:34,656 --> 00:48:35,997
[SPEAKER_00]: That's as much as you can get from it.

658
00:48:38,859 --> 00:48:45,163
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you remove the attitude towards wanting more of it, you remove any basis for spreading of sensuality.

659
00:48:47,434 --> 00:48:51,536
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no spreading of sensuality, sensuality will cease, because it requires maintenance.

660
00:48:53,197 --> 00:48:54,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Avijja requires maintenance.

661
00:48:55,278 --> 00:48:58,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance requires maintenance.

662
00:49:01,360 --> 00:49:01,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Ill-will.

663
00:49:02,961 --> 00:49:09,924
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is a thought of, I've been offended, he said something rude to me, or, oh, these people might come and kill me.

664
00:49:11,945 --> 00:49:16,007
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not trying to get rid of the displeasure of that thought,

665
00:49:17,732 --> 00:49:20,114
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be affected by anything that happens through that port.

666
00:49:21,075 --> 00:49:24,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And you certainly won't be able to engage with anything unwholesome.

667
00:49:28,661 --> 00:49:31,123
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you wouldn't be able to kill somebody even in self-defense.

668
00:49:32,044 --> 00:49:32,644
[SPEAKER_01]: You just wouldn't.

669
00:49:33,385 --> 00:49:34,346
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to defend.

670
00:49:46,203 --> 00:49:49,145
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is pain not wanted and why is pleasure wanted?

671
00:49:52,828 --> 00:49:55,950
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about getting rid of the pleasure or having more of it.

672
00:49:55,990 --> 00:49:58,432
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about getting rid of the pain, making it never appear.

673
00:49:59,513 --> 00:50:03,196
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about not wanting one and not trying to get rid of the other.

674
00:50:03,456 --> 00:50:03,836
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

675
00:50:07,098 --> 00:50:13,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can't say... You have a say, you have a thought, as we said, of eating some tasty food.

676
00:50:14,759 --> 00:50:25,543
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you're full-on chugging food down your throat, just swallowing it, and saying to yourself, but see, I don't want it on the level of my thought.

677
00:50:25,603 --> 00:50:26,483
[SPEAKER_01]: That's inconceivable.

678
00:50:26,764 --> 00:50:28,064
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a contradiction in terms.

679
00:50:29,024 --> 00:50:37,027
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I would have said it's impossible to engage physically with sensuality without having sensual perceptions on the level of thought.

680
00:50:37,628 --> 00:50:38,208
[SPEAKER_01]: You just can't.

681
00:50:40,789 --> 00:50:43,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, the sensory strain, the physical restraint, needs to come first.

682
00:50:46,492 --> 00:50:49,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's based on that wrong discrepancy.

683
00:50:50,237 --> 00:50:53,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through engaging with it, you're affirming the discrepancy.

684
00:50:54,522 --> 00:50:57,506
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're affirming the discrepancy, you're affirming suffering.

685
00:50:58,808 --> 00:51:02,052
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you get rid of suffering when you're affirming it through your own very actions?

686
00:51:26,910 --> 00:51:28,071
[SPEAKER_00]: You're building up on suffering.

687
00:51:29,212 --> 00:51:31,574
[SPEAKER_00]: You take suffering as your foundation.

688
00:51:31,694 --> 00:51:32,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

689
00:51:35,436 --> 00:51:40,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means any of your efforts cannot really pertain to suffering, to undoing of it.

690
00:51:42,742 --> 00:51:44,163
[SPEAKER_01]: But covering, trying to cover it up.

691
00:51:44,884 --> 00:51:45,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to get rid of it.

692
00:51:47,706 --> 00:51:52,029
[SPEAKER_01]: But trying to get rid of it is perpetuation of the attitude that creates the problem.

693
00:51:52,389 --> 00:51:52,910
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I meant.

694
00:51:53,590 --> 00:51:55,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Your attitude creates the problem and problem perpetuates it.

695
00:51:57,095 --> 00:51:57,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Once created.

696
00:51:58,376 --> 00:52:02,659
[SPEAKER_01]: And... but there is no first point where that problem wasn't... was created and wasn't before.

697
00:52:03,100 --> 00:52:04,381
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no first point to avidya.

698
00:52:10,686 --> 00:52:12,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can just think about pain, as we started.

699
00:52:13,167 --> 00:52:22,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you remove the attitude of not wanting pain, you're automatically dealing with sensuality, with pleasure, and with pain, and with ill-will.

700
00:52:25,217 --> 00:52:26,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you would give in to sensuality,

701
00:52:27,694 --> 00:52:29,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's unpleasant to not do so.

702
00:52:31,996 --> 00:52:35,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the way of getting rid of that displeasure is by doing so.

703
00:52:37,019 --> 00:52:40,060
[SPEAKER_01]: But, what if you're not affected by that displeasure in the first place?

704
00:52:41,901 --> 00:52:44,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you realize, oh, there's nothing for me to get rid of.

705
00:52:44,243 --> 00:52:45,623
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing for me to get engaged with.

706
00:52:55,308 --> 00:52:56,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionality is pleasant.

707
00:52:56,940 --> 00:52:57,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you've given in to it.

708
00:53:01,882 --> 00:53:04,423
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to look as to why did you have to give in to it?

709
00:53:04,964 --> 00:53:06,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it was painful.

710
00:53:08,725 --> 00:53:15,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And everybody knows that.

711
00:53:19,170 --> 00:53:22,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what... like what we spoke before, like the willpower and so on.

712
00:53:22,091 --> 00:53:23,492
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you should use willpower.

713
00:53:24,762 --> 00:53:29,524
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like saying no to everything and overcoming and making it like, you know, prevailing over every single thing.

714
00:53:29,544 --> 00:53:29,624
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

715
00:53:30,904 --> 00:53:41,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Just find things on account of which you suffer, which is your attitude in regard to pleasure, your attitude in regard to pain, your attitude in regard to neither pleasure nor pain.

716
00:53:43,169 --> 00:53:44,130
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, prevail there.

717
00:53:44,290 --> 00:53:44,650
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

718
00:53:45,830 --> 00:53:52,733
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to worry about anything else then, because whatever you do won't be unwholesome, because it's only rooted in trying to get rid of the pain.

719
00:53:55,670 --> 00:53:57,391
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not rooted in the discrepancy.

720
00:54:00,174 --> 00:54:09,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, it's our own ignorance, and our own actions rooted in it, that are perpetuating it, that is obscuring the nature of things as they actually are.

721
00:54:13,624 --> 00:54:21,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove your discrepant attitude, or attitude that results in discrepancy, that creates it, that generates it,

722
00:54:22,430 --> 00:54:23,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you see the way things are.

723
00:54:24,192 --> 00:54:26,935
[SPEAKER_01]: In the seen, there's just the seen.

724
00:54:27,275 --> 00:54:28,336
[SPEAKER_01]: There never was anything more.

725
00:54:28,596 --> 00:54:29,798
[SPEAKER_01]: There couldn't have been anything more.

726
00:54:31,199 --> 00:54:32,981
[SPEAKER_01]: In the felt, there was just the felt.

727
00:54:33,882 --> 00:54:35,624
[SPEAKER_01]: In the perceived, there was the perceived.

728
00:54:36,565 --> 00:54:38,006
[SPEAKER_01]: In the intended, there was the intended.

729
00:54:39,007 --> 00:54:39,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

730
00:54:43,812 --> 00:54:45,294
[SPEAKER_01]: In the world, there was just the world.

731
00:55:02,792 --> 00:55:11,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, you can truly learn from your pain, whatever that pain is.

732
00:55:11,255 --> 00:55:17,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through, like, psychologizing and developing some nice, pleasing theories, hence your learning, inverted commas.

733
00:55:18,398 --> 00:55:18,578
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

734
00:55:19,498 --> 00:55:27,661
[SPEAKER_01]: With presence of pain, you automatically, simultaneously, must have presence of that gratuitous attitude, unless you're fully enlightened.

735
00:55:31,668 --> 00:55:37,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through presence of disagreeable, you have a basis for undoing your attitude towards the disagreeable.

736
00:55:39,093 --> 00:55:44,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, yeah, pain is a great teacher, if done rightly, if you know how to look at it, if you have a right view.

737
00:56:01,075 --> 00:56:16,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you know where to look, then all you have to do is practice composure, self-control, restraint, because all these things will be revealing the nature of feelings that are experienced unpleasantly.

738
00:56:17,103 --> 00:56:22,386
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be revealing the displeasure of sensuality, the displeasure of ill-will.

739
00:56:22,406 --> 00:56:24,387
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll be revealing it, not creating it.

740
00:56:26,468 --> 00:56:27,349
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you reveal the

741
00:56:27,943 --> 00:56:35,368
[SPEAKER_01]: The displeasure that was making you have the attitude towards getting rid of the displeasure, you start to undo the attitude automatically.

742
00:56:40,532 --> 00:56:44,214
[SPEAKER_00]: That work can be in front of you, always.

743
00:56:44,374 --> 00:56:45,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

744
00:56:45,495 --> 00:56:45,795
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

745
00:56:45,935 --> 00:56:50,879
[SPEAKER_01]: It is always, because it's always something felt, which means there's always attitude in regard to whatever's being felt.

746
00:56:53,978 --> 00:56:55,860
[SPEAKER_00]: You say that's the only work that needs to be done.

747
00:56:55,880 --> 00:56:57,001
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only work.

748
00:56:57,241 --> 00:56:58,822
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the work on the most fundamental level.

749
00:56:59,323 --> 00:57:04,287
[SPEAKER_01]: If you fix things on the most fundamental level, everything else built on top of it will be fixed equally.

750
00:57:04,307 --> 00:57:14,436
[SPEAKER_01]: If you purify the stream at its very origin, top of the mountain, eventually, even the rivers and seas that are being filled by that stream will be purified.

751
00:57:15,436 --> 00:57:21,562
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can be battling and trying to purify the rivers down there, down the mountain, while there is still the source of pollution remains,

752
00:57:24,798 --> 00:57:29,460
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how, you know, when the Buddha said he purifies his mind from unwholesome thoughts, from thoughts of sensuality.

753
00:57:29,661 --> 00:57:30,481
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly how it's done.

754
00:57:30,521 --> 00:57:32,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by chasing every individual.

755
00:57:32,802 --> 00:57:33,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I was thinking this.

756
00:57:33,843 --> 00:57:34,803
[SPEAKER_01]: What did I mean?

757
00:57:34,843 --> 00:57:35,003
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

758
00:57:36,044 --> 00:57:36,864
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of this?

759
00:57:37,525 --> 00:57:41,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Was it rooted in trying to get rid of the pain caused by sensual pleasure?

760
00:57:42,347 --> 00:57:43,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Prospect of sensual pleasure?

761
00:57:43,948 --> 00:57:47,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Was it rooted in trying to get rid of the pain caused by something disagreeable?

762
00:57:49,211 --> 00:57:51,352
[SPEAKER_01]: Was it trying to get rid of the pain caused by something agreeable?

763
00:57:51,992 --> 00:57:53,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Was it trying to get rid of the pain caused by neutral?

764
00:57:54,876 --> 00:57:58,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, it's going to be either sensuality, ill-will or delusion.

765
00:57:59,683 --> 00:58:01,906
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he doesn't entertain it on that level.

766
00:58:02,768 --> 00:58:05,592
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how he purifies his behavior in every other regard.

